{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"homeschool-launch-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Homeschool launch track","description":"Takes a family from deciding to homeschool to a working first term in gated steps, from obligations to check and an approach to first weeks, a first-term plan, records and a review.","category":"homeschooling","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["discover","plan","build"],"role":["parent"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["preferences","notes"],"output":["plan","checklist","table"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"beginner","depth":"basic","tags":["getting-started","first-term","new-homeschoolers","leaving-school","starter-plan"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["choose-homeschool-approach","plan-deschooling-period","plan-homeschool-year","design-homeschool-record-system"],"personas":["homeschool-mentor"]},"args":[{"name":"family","description":"Each child's age, current schooling, level in reading and maths, interests and needs; who will teach and when.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"reasons","description":"Why you want to homeschool, in your own words, and any urgency.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"location","description":"Optional: your country, state or region.","type":"string"}],"steps":[{"id":"situation","file":"steps/01-situation.md","stage":"discover","gate":"approve","artifact":"homeschool-launch/01-situation.md"},{"id":"approach","file":"steps/02-approach.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"homeschool-launch/02-approach.md"},{"id":"term","file":"steps/03-first-term.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"homeschool-launch/03-first-term.md"},{"id":"records","file":"steps/04-records.md","stage":"build","gate":"none","artifact":"homeschool-launch/04-records.md"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Takes a family from \"we are going to homeschool\" to a working first term. It gathers the facts and obligations to check, chooses a starting approach, plans the first weeks and a first term, and sets up records and a review date. Each step writes one artifact and waits for approval.\n\n<family>\n{{family}}\n</family>\n\n<reasons>\n{{reasons}}\n</reasons>\n\n{{#location}}Where we live: {{location}}{{/location}}\n\nRules for every step:\n- Never state what the law requires anywhere. Turn legal and registration questions into a checklist for the official education authority.\n- Use only what the family has said; ask for missing essentials and mark gaps as [X].\n- Start smaller than the family wants; a plan that survives the first term beats an ambitious one dropped by week four.\n- Put the child's wellbeing first. If anything suggests distress, self-harm or a safeguarding concern, say so and point to a doctor or the right service before going on.\n- Do not shame schools, the child or the parents.\n- End each artifact with open questions.","steps":[{"id":"situation","file":"steps/01-situation.md","stage":"discover","gate":"approve","artifact":"homeschool-launch/01-situation.md","text":"# Step 1: Situation and obligations\n\n1. Summarise each child: age, where they are in reading and maths, interests, needs, and how they feel about leaving or never starting school.\n2. Summarise the family: who teaches, when, work commitments, budget, and the reasons for homeschooling in the family's own words.\n3. Write the obligations checklist: questions to confirm with the official authority (notification or registration, deregistration from a current school, required subjects, records, reviews, deadlines), and who usually answers each.\n4. Flag anything urgent: a child currently refusing school, a deadline, a child with additional needs who may lose support, or wellbeing concerns.\n\nSections: Children, Family, Obligations to confirm (table: question | who to ask | deadline if known), Urgent flags, Open questions.\n\nStop and wait for approval."},{"id":"approach","file":"steps/02-approach.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"homeschool-launch/02-approach.md","text":"# Step 2: Approach and first weeks\n\n1. From step 1, shortlist two approaches or a mix (for example structured, classical, Charlotte Mason, unit studies, project-based, interest-led) with why each fits these children and this parent.\n2. Decide whether to begin with a deschooling period. If yes, give its provisional length and light rhythm; if no, say why.\n3. Plan the first two to four weeks: a gentle daily rhythm, two or three core skills only, read-alouds, outings, and one thing each child is excited about.\n4. List what to buy or borrow now and what to wait on until the approach is tested.\n\nSections: Approach shortlist, Deschooling decision, First weeks (table: day | rhythm), Buy now and wait, Open questions.\n\nStop and wait for approval."},{"id":"term","file":"steps/03-first-term.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"homeschool-launch/03-first-term.md","text":"# Step 3: First term plan\n\n1. Set three to five goals per child for the term, in reading, writing, maths and one or two interest areas.\n2. Choose resources per subject, preferring what the family has, the library and free options; mark each to test before buying more.\n3. Write the weekly rhythm with teaching blocks, independent work, outside activities and social contact, fitted to the parents' real hours.\n4. Add the wider learning: co-op, clubs, outings, physical activity, and friendships.\n5. Name the likely sticking points for this family and a fallback for bad days.\n\nSections: Goals by child, Resources (table: subject | resource | status), Weekly rhythm, Wider learning, Sticking points and fallback, Open questions.\n\nStop and wait for approval."},{"id":"records","file":"steps/04-records.md","stage":"build","gate":"none","artifact":"homeschool-launch/04-records.md","text":"# Step 4: Records and review\n\n1. Set up light records that match the obligations checklist: a daily log line, a reading list, dated samples kept monthly, and a short weekly note.\n2. Set the review date at the end of the first term with the questions to ask: what progressed, what stalled, how each child feels, how the parents are coping.\n3. Write the support list: local or online home education groups to look for, and when to involve a doctor, assessor or the authority.\n4. Write a one-page summary of the whole plan the family can stick on the fridge.\n\nSections: Records, Term review, Support, One-page summary, Open questions."}]}
